SPECIAL ISSUE ARTICLE
Biostatistics deserts and scholarly productivity among US
medical schools
Terrie Vasilopoulos PhD 1 | Dmitry Tumin PhD 2
1Department of Anesthesiology and Department of Orthopaedic Surgery and Sports Medicine, University of Florida College of Medicine, Gainesville, Florida, USA
2Department of Pediatrics and Department of Academic Affairs, Brody School of Medicine at East Carolina University, Greenville, North Carolina, USA
Correspondence
Terrie Vasilopoulos, Department of Anesthesiology and Department of Orthopaedic Surgery and Sports Medicine, University of Florida College of Medicine, Gainesville, FL, USA. Email: tvasilopoulos@anest.ufl.edu
The teaching and practice of biostatistics are essential to medical education, but
access to biostatistical expertise is limited at many medical schools. Medical schools
in rural and historically underserved areas may be doubly disadvantaged in accessing
biostatistics expertise by their lack of financial resources and a lack of local
programmes training future generations of biostatisticians. Using public data on US
medical schools and biostatistics PhD programmes, we identified medical schools
operating in ‘biostatistics deserts’ (institutions without an affiliated or colocated PhD
programme in biostatistics) and correlated each medical school's location in a biosta
tistics desert with scholarly productivity, operationalized as the annual number of
scholarly publications. Among 126 MD-granting medical schools in our analysis, 46%
were located in a biostatistics desert and had a median of 590 publications/year,
compared to 993 for medical schools with a colocated PhD programme and 1,369
with an affiliated programme. On multivariable analysis, the presence of a Biostatis
tics, Epidemiology, and Research Design (BERD) programme, but not affiliation or
colocation with a biostatistics PhD program, was associated with higher scholarly
productivity. Structured biostatistics services, such as BERD programmes, may repre
sent the best opportunity for medical schools to leverage the local biostatistics work
force to support scholarly publication.
1 | INTRODUCTION
With the increasing methodological complexity of biomedical research, the teaching and practice of biostatistics have become essential to the
educational and research missions of medical schools and academic medical centres (AMCs) (Welty et al., 2013). However, medical trainees fre
quently cite insufficient access to biostatistics expertise: in 2022, almost 3 in 10 graduating physicians described their preclinical training in biosta
tistics and epidemiology as fair or poor (Association of American Medical Colleges, 2022a), and surveys of resident physicians revealed that
approximately half consider a lack of biostatistics knowledge or the absence of biostatistical support as barriers to participating in scholarly activ
ity (Abramson et al., 2018; Mansi et al., 2019). The shortage of biostatisticians is a fundamental cause of limited biostatistics support for medical
trainees, and this shortage begins with the biostatistician training pipeline. For example, in 2021, only 222 doctoral degrees in biostatistics were
awarded in the United States (American Statistical Association, n.d.-b), as compared to 28,337 MD and DO degrees (Kaiser Family Foundation, n.
d.). While some medical schools have sought to address this shortage by leveraging the expertise of master's-level biostatisticians and PhDs in
related fields (Desai et al., 2022; McKinney et al., 2019), the rarity of biostatistics PhDs relative to physicians is emblematic of a nationwide imbal
ance between biostatistician supply and demand.
The nationwide shortage of biostatisticians is exacerbated by inequality among medical schools in access to biostatistics resources. In 2010
2013, a quarter of US medical schools had four or fewer biostatistics faculty, and a quarter of ‘small’ schools (those with <700 faculty) had just a
single biostatistician faculty member (Zhang & Chen, 2015). While some medical schools and AMCs benefit from in-house biostatistics cores
funded by the National Institutes of Health (NIH) (Hanlon et al., 2022), such cores are absent at many medical schools due to the highly unequal
