distribution of NIH funding across institutions. Medical schools in rural and historically underserved areas may be doubly disadvantaged in
accessing biostatistics expertise because of their lack of financial resources (e.g., NIH and other funding for biostatistics cores) and a lack of local
programmes training future generations of biostatisticians. We refer to the latter disadvantage as the phenomenon of ‘biostatistics deserts’,
mirroring the concept of ‘food deserts’ that describes areas lacking in retailers of nutritious foods (Testa et al., 2021).
Medical schools operating in a biostatistics desert may encounter several barriers to teaching and research involving biostatistical methods.
First, these schools may encounter difficulty recruiting biostatistics faculty and staff due to the absence of a local talent pool. Second, any biostat
isticians who are recruited are at risk of isolation, which could lead to lower productivity, career dissatisfaction, and departure from the institution
(Mehta et al., 2022). Third, schools in biostatistics deserts would be unable to draw upon support from biostatistics trainees (e.g., biostatistics stu
dent interns and student-led consulting services) (Kaseda et al., 2022; Pomann et al., 2022). Altogether, these barriers may exacerbate disparities
among US medical schools in scholarly productivity, as described in prior research (Gray et al., 2023). In this study, we aimed to define how many
US medical schools are located in a biostatistics desert, defined by the absence of a colocated or affiliated biostatistics training program. Our sec
ondary aim was to describe the correlation between biostatistics deserts and medical schools' research productivity, measured through the num
ber of scholarly publications.
2 | METHODS
This study did not include human subjects research and did not require Institutional Review Board approval. We included US-based MD-granting
medical schools, identified by querying the Association of American Medical Colleges, AAMC (n.d.) website. We excluded regional campuses,
which may have had access to biostatistics support through their affiliation with the flagship campus of the same school. Following prior research
(Gray et al., 2023; Tumin, Khanchandani, et al., 2022), we also excluded medical schools not uniquely identifiable by affiliation search in the
Scopus database, as well as schools missing data on any other study variables (including schools not represented in the Blue Ridge Institute for
Medical Research [BRIMR] summary of NIH funding).
For each medical school, the primary outcome was the availability of a local biostatistics training programme. This was defined based on a
directory of graduate programmes maintained by the American Statistical Association, ASA (n.d.-a). Our primary definition of this outcome was
based on the availability of a PhD programme in biostatistics. In secondary analyses, we expanded this definition to include (a) PhD programmes
in either statistics or biostatistics and (b) master's or PhD programmes in either statistics or biostatistics. For each definition, we classified medical
schools as having (a) an affiliated training programme (housed in the same institution as the medical school); (b) a colocated training programme
(housed in a different institution, but located in the same county as the medical school's flagship campus); or (c) neither. Medical schools with nei
ther an affiliated nor a colocated biostatistics training programme were considered to be located in a biostatistics desert. Our secondary outcome
was each medical school's number of scholarly publications in 2022, determined by a search of the Scopus database.
Covariates in our analysis included each school's public or private designation, geographic region (based on US Census categories), and
number of full-time faculty as of December 2022 (Association of American Medical Colleges, 2022b). We also controlled for total funding
received in fiscal year 2022 from the NIH (recoded to $50 million dollar increments), as compiled by Blue Ridge Institute for Medical
Research (BRIMR, 2023). Lastly, we controlled for the presence of Biostatistics, Epidemiology and Research Design (BERD) programmes asso
ciated with Clinical and Translational Science Awards (CTSA) programme funding through the NIH's National Center for Advancing Transla
tional Sciences.
Continuous data were summarized using medians with interquartile ranges, while categorical data were summarized using counts with per
centages. We grouped medical schools based on access to biostatistics training programmes (those with affiliated biostatistics training programs,
those with colocated programmes and those with no local programs) to describe prevalence and geographic clustering of medical schools in bio
statistics deserts. We compared study covariates and access to biostatistics training programmes using chi-square tests, Fisher's exact tests or
Wilcoxon/Kruskal–Wallis tests, as appropriate. We compared the number of publications across the categories of medical school access to biosta
tistics training programmes, as well as individual study covariates, using negative binomial regression (due to overdispersion and skewness in dis
tribution), with associations quantified using incident rate ratios (IRRs) with 95% confidence intervals (95% CIs). Lastly, we fit a multivariable
negative binomial regression model for the number of publications including access to biostatistics training programmes and study covariates. Col
linearity was assessed prior to creating multivariable models. Data analysis was performed in JMP Pro 17 (SAS Institute Inc., Cary, NC), and
P < .05 was considered statistically significant.
