4 | DISCUSSION
This study demonstrates the existence of biostatistics deserts affecting nearly half of US-based MD-granting medical schools. Biostatistics deserts
are associated with geographical region, medical school size, private or public affiliation and NIH funding. Medical school affiliation or colocation
with a biostatistics PhD programme was associated with much greater school-wide publication productivity in bivariate analysis, although the
association between affiliated biostatistics PhD programmes and publications was attenuated after accounting for the presence of a BERD pro
gramme. This suggests that the mere presence of a biostatistics training programme is not sufficient to support biomedical research and that there
needs to be group of biostatistical and research experts with a focus and understanding of biomedical research to best support clinical faculty
research endeavours.
