to guide research question creation, protocol development and review, study design and results dissemination. Moreover, a BERD programme
can provide key education and mentoring for clinical faculty to help their professional development as researchers. The specialized expertise that
a BERD programme can provide is a key component of success at academic health centres (Welty et al., 2013). Furthermore, specialization can
refer to types of studies (e.g., comparative effectiveness research and clinical trials) and methodologies (e.g., predictive modelling and imaging
data) with which a given biostatistician is more experienced. Moreover, specialization can also refer to medical specialties where a biostatistician
maintains a long-term relationship with clinical faculty for specific areas, thus becoming familiar with specialty-specific variables and processes.
This can support formation of investigator-focused professional relationships, as opposed to project-based relationships (Rubio et al., 2011;
Tsevat et al., 2014; Welty et al., 2013).
Despite the documented success of BERD programmes in providing comprehensive and effective statistical expertise, additional strategies
for optimizing biostatistical support are needed for historically under-resourced schools (particularly schools that currently lack a BERD pro
gramme or a significant portfolio of NIH funding) and schools located in the biostatistics deserts described in our study. In a recent survey of col
laborative biostatistics units, 21% included no NIH-funded cores, 14% received no grant funding from the NIH and the median team included
only three total members (Hanlon et al., 2022). Small, agile teams of biostatisticians and other research faculty or staff can maximize their impact
on medical schools' research productivity by (a) developing a model of shared responsibilities for research mentorship and research operations,
(b) proactively engaging clinical faculty and trainees in research to develop the local pool of investigators and (c) leveraging team members' diverse
academic backgrounds, which may include exposure to statistical methods through training in academic disciplines not traditionally associated
with the biostatistician role (Tumin, Baumgarten, et al., 2022; Tumin et al., 2020).
In addition to biostatistical support, several other factors were associated with medical school publications in our study, including NIH
funding and region. A study dating back four decades supported a strong association between publications and NIH funding (McAllister &
Narin, 1983). More recent studies have confirmed that relationship and found that publications supported by NIH funding have a greater
impact in terms of journal prestige and number of citations (Boyack & Jordan, 2011; Lewison & Dawson, 1998). Nonetheless, our unadjusted
analysis indicated that a $50 million increase in annual NIH funding was correlated with a 30% increase in annual publications, equivalent to
242 publications for the median medical school. Further work may consider how different sources of intramural or extramural funding corre
spond to the quantity and quality of resulting publications and how these data can be used to inform budgeting for biostatistical and other
research support based on targets for scholarly productivity. Interestingly, the aforementioned 1983 study also found an association between
publications and medical school region, with fewer publications from Southern US-based schools (McAllister & Narin, 1983). Our study found
similar regional differences in publication output, with the highest output from Northeastern US-based schools and lowest outputs from
Southern and Western US-based schools, supporting the persistence of regional disparity in terms of academically productive medical
schools.
Our conclusions are limited by some aspects of the available data and the analytic approach. The ASA registry of graduate programmes may
not have included all eligible training programmes in statistics and biostatistics, resulting in overestimation of the proportion of medical schools
located in a biostatistics desert. We also did not examine affiliation or colocation of graduate programmes in other disciplines that include signifi
cant training in biostatistical methods. Our ascertainment of publication outcomes shares the limitation of other studies using the Scopus data
base (Gray et al., 2023), including delayed indexing of some publications and the potential for misattributing author affiliations or undercounting
publications in non-indexed outlets. While we focused on scholarly publication as a highly visible output of biostatistical support, we were unable
to quantify other contributions of biostatistical faculty and staff at each medical school, such as contributions to teaching, funding and career
development of other faculty. In our multivariable analysis, it is also possible that the inclusion of BERD programme and NIH funding independent
variables may have led to overcontrolling the model, since graduates of local biostatistics programmes may have been the biostatisticians staffing
BERD programmes and contributing to the success of each medical school's NIH funding applications.
Despite these limitations, our study provides a novel analysis of access to biostatistics resources across US medical schools and reveals that a
large share of MD-granting medical schools is operating in biostatistics deserts, where the capacity to develop and grow biostatistics units may be
limited by the lack of local graduate programmes. Our findings further suggest that structured biostatistics services, such as the BERD
programmes, may represent the best opportunity for medical schools to leverage the local biostatistics workforce to support scholarly publication;
however, they also point to the need for more research on how biostatistics units should best be organized at smaller, geographically isolated or
historically under-resourced medical schools.
